1
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:08,920
Chapter 11 A new chapter in a novel is something like a new scene in a play.

2
00:00:10,030 --> 00:00:15,180
And when I draw up the curtain this time, reader, you must fancy you see a room in

3
00:00:15,181 --> 00:00:18,077
the George Inn at milk-coat, with such large

4
00:00:18,078 --> 00:00:21,540
figured papering on the walls as in rooms have.

5
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:26,860
Such a carpet, such furniture, such ornaments on the mantelpiece,

6
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:32,459
such prints, including a portrait of George III, and another of the Prince of

7
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:35,719
Wales, and a representation of the death of Wolf.

8
00:00:36,965 --> 00:00:41,940
All this is visible to you by the light of an oil lamp hanging from the ceiling.

9
00:00:43,149 --> 00:00:49,159
And by that of an excellent fire, near which I sit in my cloak and bonnet,

10
00:00:50,299 --> 00:00:55,579
my muff and umbrella lie on the table, and I am warming away the numbness and

11
00:00:55,581 --> 00:01:00,340
chill contracted by 16 hours exposure to the rawness of an October day.

12
00:01:02,329 --> 00:01:05,040
I left Loten at 4 o'clock AM, and the milk

13
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:08,861
-coat town-clock is now just striking eight.

14
00:01:09,899 --> 00:01:15,800
Reader, though I look comfortably accommodated, I am not very tranquil in my mind.

15
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:20,959
I thought, when the coach stopped here, there would be someone to meet me.

16
00:01:21,809 --> 00:01:27,239
I looked anxiously round as I descended the wooden steps, the boots placed for my

17
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:32,500
convenience, expecting to hear my name pronounced, and to see some description of

18
00:01:32,501 --> 00:01:35,479
carriage waiting to convey me to Thornfield.

19
00:01:36,559 --> 00:01:41,139
Nothing of the sort was visible, and when I asked a waiter if anyone had

20
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:45,239
been to inquire after a miss air, I was answered in the negative,

21
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:50,139
so I had no resource but to request to be shown into a private room.

22
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:57,001
And here I am waiting while all sorts of doubts and fears are troubling my thoughts.

23
00:01:57,979 --> 00:02:03,739
It is a very strange sensation, to inexperienced youth, to feel itself

24
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:08,239
quite alone in the world, cut a drift from every connection.

25
00:02:08,959 --> 00:02:14,360
Uncertain whether the port to which it is bound can be reached, and prevented by

26
00:02:14,361 --> 00:02:18,139
many impediments, from returning to that it has quitted.

27
00:02:19,209 --> 00:02:24,920
The charm of adventure sweetens that sensation, the glow of pride warms it,

28
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:31,000
but then the throb of fear disturbs it, and fear with me, became predominant,

29
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:34,659
when half an hour elapsed, and still I was alone.

30
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:38,379
I bet thought myself to ring the bell.

31
00:02:39,969 --> 00:02:43,305
Is there a place in this neighborhood called Thornfield,

32
00:02:43,306 --> 00:02:45,940
I asked, of the waiter who answered the summons.

33
00:02:46,899 --> 00:02:47,899
Thornfield?

34
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:50,180
I don't know mum, I'll inquire at the bar.

35
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:54,040
He vanished, but reappeared instantly.

36
00:02:55,290 --> 00:02:56,290
Is your name air miss?

37
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:58,440
Yes.

38
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:00,520
Person here waiting for you?

39
00:03:01,569 --> 00:03:06,500
I jumped up, took my muff and umbrella, and hastened into the inn passage.

40
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:10,923
A man was standing by the open door, and in the

41
00:03:10,924 --> 00:03:14,879
lamplit street, I dimly saw a one -horse conveyance.

42
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:20,585
This will be your luggage, I suppose, said the man, rather

43
00:03:20,586 --> 00:03:24,340
abruptly when he saw me, pointing to my trunk in the passage.

44
00:03:25,319 --> 00:03:26,319
Yes.

45
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:32,081
He hoisted it onto the vehicle, which was a sort of car, and then I got in.

46
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:36,180
Before he shut me up, I asked him how far it was to Thornfield.

47
00:03:37,729 --> 00:03:38,819
A matter of six miles.

48
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,239
How long shall we be before we get there?

49
00:03:42,299 --> 00:03:43,360
Happen an hour and a half?

50
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:49,120
He fastened the car door, climbed to his own seat outside, and we set off.

51
00:03:50,559 --> 00:03:54,719
Our progress was leisurely, and gave me ample time to reflect.

52
00:03:55,704 --> 00:04:01,420
I was content to be at length so near the end of my journey, and as I leaned back in

53
00:04:01,421 --> 00:04:06,799
the comfortable, though not elegant conveyance, I meditated much at my ease.

54
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:14,004
I suppose, thought I, judging from the planeness of the servant and carriage, Mrs.

55
00:04:14,804 --> 00:04:17,139
Fairfax is not a very dashing person.

56
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:19,139
So much the better.

57
00:04:20,079 --> 00:04:25,340
I never lived amongst fine people but once, and I was very miserable with them.

58
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:29,000
I wonder if she lives alone except this little girl?

59
00:04:29,809 --> 00:04:35,879
If so, and if she is in any degree amiable, I shall surely be able to get on with her.

60
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:37,340
I will do my best.

61
00:04:37,879 --> 00:04:41,899
It is a pity that doing one's best does not always answer.

62
00:04:42,989 --> 00:04:48,399
At lower indeed, I took that resolution, kept it, and succeeded in pleasing.

63
00:04:49,315 --> 00:04:54,120
But with Mrs. Reed, I remember my best was always spurned with scorn.

64
00:04:55,350 --> 00:05:00,600
I pray God Mrs. Fairfax may not turn out a second Mrs. Reed, but if she does,

65
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:03,219
I am not bound to stay with her.

66
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:07,559
Let the worst come to the worst, I can advertise again.

67
00:05:09,530 --> 00:05:12,059
How far are we on our road now, I wonder?

68
00:05:13,299 --> 00:05:15,200
I let down the window and looked out.

69
00:05:16,059 --> 00:05:19,700
Millcoat was behind us, judging by the number of its lights.

70
00:05:20,479 --> 00:05:24,399
It seemed a place of considerable magnitude much larger than Loughton.

71
00:05:25,030 --> 00:05:28,945
We were now as far as I could see on a sort of common,

72
00:05:28,947 --> 00:05:31,700
but there were houses scattered all over the district.

73
00:05:32,510 --> 00:05:36,255
I felt we were in a different region to Loughwood, more

74
00:05:36,256 --> 00:05:40,600
populous, less picturesque, more stirring, less romantic.

75
00:05:41,959 --> 00:05:48,260
The roads were heavy, the night misty, my conductor let his horse walk all the

76
00:05:48,261 --> 00:05:53,079
way, and the hour and a half extended, I verily believe, to two hours.

77
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:59,459
At last he turned in his seat and said, you're known so far for a thornfield now.

78
00:06:01,139 --> 00:06:02,399
Again I looked out.

79
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:04,760
We were passing a church.

80
00:06:05,659 --> 00:06:11,479
I saw its low broad tower against the sky, and its bell was tolling a quarter.

81
00:06:12,559 --> 00:06:18,620
I saw a narrow galaxy of lights, too, on a hillside, marking a village or hamlet.

82
00:06:19,159 --> 00:06:23,059
About ten minutes after, the driver got down and opened a pair of gates.

83
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:26,739
We passed through, and they clashed to behind us.

84
00:06:27,450 --> 00:06:32,500
We now slowly ascended a drive, and came upon the long front of a house.

85
00:06:33,219 --> 00:06:38,079
Candle-like gleamed from one curtained bow window, all the rest were dark.

86
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:40,940
The car stopped at the front door.

87
00:06:41,579 --> 00:06:45,180
It was opened by a maid servant, I elited and went in.

88
00:06:45,639 --> 00:06:49,522
Will you walk this way, ma'am?" said the girl, and I followed

89
00:06:49,523 --> 00:06:53,280
her across a square hall, with high doors all around.

90
00:06:54,309 --> 00:06:59,399
She ushered me into a room, whose double illumination of fire and candle,

91
00:06:59,985 --> 00:07:03,370
at first dazzled me, contrasting as it did with the

92
00:07:03,371 --> 00:07:07,139
darkness to which my eyes had been for two hours inured.

93
00:07:07,750 --> 00:07:14,200
When I could see, however, a cozy and agreeable picture presented itself to my view.

94
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:21,879
A snug small room, a round table by a cheerful fire, an armchair high backed an

95
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:27,659
old -fashioned, wherein sat the neatest imaginable little elderly lady in widow's

96
00:07:27,661 --> 00:07:34,197
cap, black silk gown, and snowy muslin apron, exactly like what I had fancied Mrs.

97
00:07:34,198 --> 00:07:34,879
Fairfax.

98
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,219
Only less stately and milder looking.

99
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,760
She was occupied in knitting, a large cat sat demurly at her feet.

100
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:49,120
Nothing in short was wanting to complete the bow ideal of domestic comfort.

101
00:07:49,959 --> 00:07:54,659
A more reassuring introduction for a new governess could scarcely be conceived.

102
00:07:55,399 --> 00:08:00,840
There was no grandeur to overwhelm, no stateliness to embarrass, and then,

103
00:08:01,264 --> 00:08:07,320
as I entered, the old lady got up and promptly and kindly came forward to meet me.

104
00:08:08,579 --> 00:08:09,719
How do you do my dear?

105
00:08:10,399 --> 00:08:12,600
I am afraid you have had a tedious ride.

106
00:08:13,219 --> 00:08:16,799
John drives so slowly, you must be cold, come to the fire.

107
00:08:18,469 --> 00:08:20,559
Mrs. Fairfax, I suppose, said I.

108
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:22,959
Yes, you are right, do sit down.

109
00:08:24,350 --> 00:08:27,498
She conducted me to her own chair, and then began

110
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:30,319
to remove my shawl and untie my bonnet strings.

111
00:08:31,299 --> 00:08:33,860
I begged she would not give herself so much trouble.

112
00:08:34,809 --> 00:08:35,979
Oh, it is no trouble.

113
00:08:36,719 --> 00:08:39,360
I dare say your own hands are almost numbed with cold.

114
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:43,496
Leah, make a little hot negus and cut a sandwich

115
00:08:43,497 --> 00:08:46,740
or two, here are the keys of the storeroom.

116
00:08:47,530 --> 00:08:51,485
And she produced from her pocket a most house-wifely

117
00:08:51,486 --> 00:08:54,361
bunch of keys and delivered them to the servant.

118
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:59,479
Now then, draw nearer to the fire, she continued.

119
00:09:00,879 --> 00:09:03,159
You've brought your luggage with you, haven't you my dear?

120
00:09:03,899 --> 00:09:04,899
Yes, ma'am.

121
00:09:05,649 --> 00:09:09,520
I'll see it carried into your room, she said, and bustled out.

122
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:13,799
She treats me like a visitor, thought I.

123
00:09:14,889 --> 00:09:16,959
I little expected such a reception.

124
00:09:18,379 --> 00:09:21,639
I anticipated only coldness and stiffness.

125
00:09:22,579 --> 00:09:25,259
This is not like what I have heard of the treatment

126
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:28,459
of governesses, but I must not exalt too soon.

127
00:09:29,889 --> 00:09:32,930
She returned, with her own hands cleared her

128
00:09:32,931 --> 00:09:36,380
knitting apparatus and a book or two from the table.

129
00:09:37,079 --> 00:09:41,105
To make room for the tray which Leah now brought,

130
00:09:41,106 --> 00:09:44,540
and then herself handed me the refreshments.

131
00:09:45,649 --> 00:09:50,600
I felt rather confused at being the object of more attention than I had ever before

132
00:09:50,601 --> 00:09:55,780
received, and that too, shown by my employer and superior.

133
00:09:57,069 --> 00:10:02,620
But as she did not herself seem to consider, she was doing anything out of her place.

134
00:10:03,239 --> 00:10:05,979
I thought it better to take her civilities quietly.

135
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:10,600
Shall I have the pleasure of seeing Miss Fairfax tonight?

136
00:10:11,569 --> 00:10:14,620
I asked when I had partaken of what she offered me.

137
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:17,760
What did you say, my dear?

138
00:10:18,604 --> 00:10:23,860
I am a little deaf, returned the good lady, approaching her ear to my mouth.

139
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:26,760
I repeated the question more distinctly.

140
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:30,319
Miss Fairfax, oh, you mean Miss Varenz?

141
00:10:30,905 --> 00:10:33,079
Varenz is the name of your future pupil.

142
00:10:34,120 --> 00:10:35,120
Indeed.

143
00:10:35,729 --> 00:10:37,020
Then she is not your daughter?

144
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:39,840
No, I have no family.

145
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:44,191
I should have followed up my first inquiry by asking

146
00:10:44,192 --> 00:10:46,700
in what way Miss Varenz was connected with her.

147
00:10:47,430 --> 00:10:49,579
But I recollected it was not polite.

148
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:52,200
To ask too many questions.

149
00:10:52,989 --> 00:10:55,719
Besides, I was sure to hear in time.

150
00:10:57,229 --> 00:10:58,579
I am so glad.

151
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:04,459
She continued, as she sat down opposite to me and took the cat on her knee.

152
00:11:05,409 --> 00:11:07,420
I am so glad you are calm.

153
00:11:08,239 --> 00:11:11,100
It will be quite pleasant living here now with a companion.

154
00:11:12,059 --> 00:11:17,399
To be sure it is pleasant at any time, for Thornfield is a fine old hall,

155
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,979
rather neglected of late years perhaps.

156
00:11:20,809 --> 00:11:23,000
But still it is a respectable place.

157
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:28,120
Yet you know in wintertime one feels dreary quite alone in the best quarters.

158
00:11:29,020 --> 00:11:30,120
I say alone.

159
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:36,800
Lea is a nice girl to be sure, and John and his wife are very decent people.

160
00:11:37,264 --> 00:11:39,852
But then you see they are only servants, and one

161
00:11:39,853 --> 00:11:42,820
can't converse with them on terms of equality.

162
00:11:43,415 --> 00:11:48,099
One must keep them at due distance, for fear of losing one's authority.

163
00:11:49,610 --> 00:11:50,959
I am sure last winter.

164
00:11:52,259 --> 00:11:54,846
It was a very severe one if you recollect,

165
00:11:54,847 --> 00:11:58,701
and when it did not snow it rained and blew.

166
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,731
Not a creature, but the butcher and postman

167
00:12:01,733 --> 00:12:05,279
came to the house from November till February.

168
00:12:06,264 --> 00:12:10,919
And I really got quite melancholy, with sitting night after night alone.

169
00:12:12,169 --> 00:12:14,752
I had Lea interread to me sometimes, but I

170
00:12:14,753 --> 00:12:18,140
don't think the poor girl liked the task much.

171
00:12:19,139 --> 00:12:20,479
She felt it confining.

172
00:12:21,950 --> 00:12:24,020
In spring and summer one got on better.

173
00:12:24,939 --> 00:12:29,860
Sunshine and long days make such a difference, and then, just at the

174
00:12:29,861 --> 00:12:34,220
commencement of this autumn, little Adelevarans came and her nurse.

175
00:12:35,450 --> 00:12:42,061
A child makes a house alive all at once, and now you are here, I shall be quite gay.

176
00:12:43,330 --> 00:12:48,979
My heart really warmed to the worthy lady as I heard her talk, and I drew my chair a

177
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:52,501
little nearer to her, and expressed my sincere wish that

178
00:12:52,502 --> 00:12:56,840
she might find my company as agreeable as she anticipated.

179
00:12:58,459 --> 00:13:02,020
But I'll not keep you sitting up late tonight," said she.

180
00:13:02,985 --> 00:13:07,240
It is on the stroke of 12 now, and you have been travelling all day.

181
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:09,520
You must feel tired.

182
00:13:10,270 --> 00:13:13,740
If you have got your feet well warmed, I'll show you your bedroom.

183
00:13:14,659 --> 00:13:17,080
I've had the room next to mine prepared for you.

184
00:13:17,599 --> 00:13:20,342
It is only a small apartment, but I thought you would

185
00:13:20,343 --> 00:13:23,119
like it better than one of the large front chambers.

186
00:13:23,939 --> 00:13:29,080
To be sure, they have finer furniture, but they are so dreary and solitary,

187
00:13:29,459 --> 00:13:30,939
I never sleep in them myself.

188
00:13:31,959 --> 00:13:35,584
I thanked her for her considerate choice, and as I really felt

189
00:13:35,585 --> 00:13:40,540
fatigued with my long journey, expressed my readiness to retire.

190
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:44,700
She took her candle, and I followed her from the room.

191
00:13:45,759 --> 00:13:49,168
First she went to see if the hall door was fastened, having

192
00:13:49,169 --> 00:13:52,819
taken the key from the lock, she led the way upstairs.

193
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:55,700
The steps and banisters were of oak.

194
00:13:56,439 --> 00:14:02,560
The staircase window was high and lattice, both it and the long gallery into which

195
00:14:02,561 --> 00:14:08,381
the bedroom doors opened, looked as if they belonged to a church rather than a house.

196
00:14:08,869 --> 00:14:12,663
A very chill and vault-like air pervaded the stairs and

197
00:14:12,664 --> 00:14:16,720
gallery, suggesting chillest ideas of space and solitude.

198
00:14:17,540 --> 00:14:21,741
And I was glad, when finally ushered into my chamber, to find

199
00:14:21,741 --> 00:14:26,959
it of small dimensions and furnished in ordinary, modern style.

200
00:14:27,950 --> 00:14:32,580
When Mrs. Fairfax had bitten me a kind good night, and I had fastened my door,

201
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:38,520
gazed leisurely round, and in some measure, it faced the eerie impression

202
00:14:38,521 --> 00:14:44,580
made by that wide hall, that dark and spacious staircase, and that long,

203
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:48,759
cold gallery by the livelier aspect of my little room.

204
00:14:49,470 --> 00:14:52,935
I remembered that, after a day of bodily fatigue

205
00:14:52,936 --> 00:14:57,400
and mental anxiety, I was now at last in safe haven.

206
00:14:58,709 --> 00:15:03,680
The impulse of gratitude swelled my heart, and I knelt down at the bedside,

207
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:06,259
and offered up thanks where thanks were due.

208
00:15:07,389 --> 00:15:13,799
Not forgetting air I rose, to implore aid on my further path, and the power of

209
00:15:13,800 --> 00:15:19,319
meriting the kindness which seemed so frankly offered me before it was earned.

210
00:15:20,130 --> 00:15:25,479
My couch had no thorns in it that night, my solitary room, no fears.

211
00:15:26,319 --> 00:15:32,799
At once weary in content, I slept soon and soundly, when I awoke, it was broad day.

212
00:15:33,819 --> 00:15:38,279
The chamber looked such a bright little place to me, as the sun shone in between

213
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:43,540
the gay blue chint's window curtains, showing papered walls and a carpeted

214
00:15:43,541 --> 00:15:47,131
floor, so unlike the bare planks and stained

215
00:15:47,132 --> 00:15:51,240
plaster of lowered that my spirits rose at the view.

216
00:15:52,520 --> 00:15:54,580
Externals have a great effect on the young.

217
00:15:55,709 --> 00:16:01,700
I thought that a fairer era of life was beginning for me, one that was to have its

218
00:16:01,701 --> 00:16:05,319
flowers and pleasures, as well as its thorns and toils.

219
00:16:06,729 --> 00:16:12,360
My faculties, roused by the change of scene, the new field offered to hope,

220
00:16:12,610 --> 00:16:14,020
seemed all a stir.

221
00:16:15,799 --> 00:16:20,459
I cannot precisely define what they expected, but it was something pleasant,

222
00:16:21,439 --> 00:16:27,000
not perhaps that day or that month, but at an indefinite future period.

223
00:16:28,534 --> 00:16:35,840
I rose, I dressed myself with care, obliged to be plain, for I had no article

224
00:16:35,841 --> 00:16:38,979
of attire that was not made with extreme simplicity.

225
00:16:39,930 --> 00:16:42,520
I was still by nature solicitous to be neat.

226
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:45,485
It was not my habit to be disregardful of

227
00:16:45,486 --> 00:16:48,980
appearance or careless of the impression I made.

228
00:16:49,060 --> 00:16:53,381
On the contrary, I ever wished to look as well as I could,

229
00:16:53,382 --> 00:16:56,200
and to please as much as my want of beauty would permit.

230
00:16:57,650 --> 00:17:00,439
I sometimes regretted that I was not handsomer.

231
00:17:01,509 --> 00:17:07,380
I sometimes wished to have rosy cheeks, a straight nose, and small cherry mouth.

232
00:17:08,729 --> 00:17:12,779
I desired to be tall, stately, and finally developed in figure.

233
00:17:13,759 --> 00:17:16,961
I felt it a misfortune that I was so little, so

234
00:17:16,962 --> 00:17:19,919
pale, and had features so irregular and so marked.

235
00:17:21,259 --> 00:17:23,319
And why had I these aspirations and these regrets?

236
00:17:24,669 --> 00:17:26,099
It would be difficult to say.

237
00:17:27,194 --> 00:17:31,130
I could not then distinctly say it to myself, yet

238
00:17:31,131 --> 00:17:34,540
I had a reason and a logical natural reason, too.

239
00:17:36,299 --> 00:17:41,919
However, when I had brushed my hair very smooth, and put on my black frock,

240
00:17:42,080 --> 00:17:47,360
which Quaker-like as it was, at least had the merit of fitting to an icity,

241
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:50,299
and adjusted my clean white tucker.

242
00:17:51,180 --> 00:17:56,380
I thought I should do respectfully enough to appear before Mrs Fairfax, and that my

243
00:17:56,381 --> 00:18:00,939
new pupil would not at least recoil from me with antipathy.

244
00:18:02,799 --> 00:18:07,099
Having opened my chamber window, and seen that I left all things straight

245
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:10,299
and neat on the toilet table, I ventured forth.

246
00:18:11,529 --> 00:18:16,360
Traversing the long and matted gallery, I descended the slippery steps of oak.

247
00:18:17,349 --> 00:18:19,020
Then I gained the hall.

248
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:21,319
I halted there a minute.

249
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:23,599
I looked at some pictures on the walls.

250
00:18:24,189 --> 00:18:30,560
One, I remember, represented a grim man in a queer ass, and one a lady with powdered

251
00:18:30,561 --> 00:18:35,500
hair and a pearl necklace, at a bronze lamp pendant from the ceiling,

252
00:18:36,259 --> 00:18:39,346
at a great clock whose case was of oak curiously

253
00:18:39,347 --> 00:18:42,200
carved an ebb and black with time and rubbing.

254
00:18:43,069 --> 00:18:46,459
Everything appeared very stately and imposing to me.

255
00:18:47,419 --> 00:18:51,319
But then, I was so little accustomed to grandeur.

256
00:18:52,349 --> 00:18:55,959
The hall door, which was half of glass, stood open.

257
00:18:56,740 --> 00:18:57,939
I stepped over the threshold.

258
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:00,659
It was a fine autumn morning.

259
00:19:01,319 --> 00:19:06,139
The early sun shone serenely on in browned groves and still green fields.

260
00:19:07,259 --> 00:19:11,680
Advancing onto the lawn, I looked up and surveyed the front of the mansion.

261
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:18,639
It was three stories high of proportions not vast, though considerable.

262
00:19:19,479 --> 00:19:22,580
A gentleman's man-a-house, not a nobleman's seat.

263
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:26,240
Battlements round the top gave it a picturesque look.

264
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:31,115
Its gray front stood out well from the background of

265
00:19:31,116 --> 00:19:34,540
a ruckery whose quarring tenets were now on the wing.

266
00:19:35,880 --> 00:19:41,240
They flew over the lawn and founds to a light in a great meadow from which these

267
00:19:41,241 --> 00:19:47,139
were separated by a sunk fence and were an array of mighty old thorn trees.

268
00:19:47,900 --> 00:19:51,450
Strong, naughty and broad as oaks at once explained

269
00:19:51,451 --> 00:19:54,620
the etymology of the mansion's designation.

270
00:19:55,569 --> 00:20:01,919
Father off were hills, not so lofty as those round lowwood, nor so craggy,

271
00:20:02,459 --> 00:20:05,200
nor so like barriers of separation from the living world.

272
00:20:06,349 --> 00:20:12,180
But yet, quiet and lonely hills enough, and seeming to embrace thornfield,

273
00:20:12,830 --> 00:20:15,905
with a seclusion I had not expected to find

274
00:20:15,906 --> 00:20:19,819
existence so near the stirring locality of millcoat.

275
00:20:21,150 --> 00:20:24,318
A little hamlet, whose roofs were blent with

276
00:20:24,319 --> 00:20:27,780
trees, straggled up the side of one of these hills.

277
00:20:28,759 --> 00:20:31,900
The church of the district stood nearer thornfield.

278
00:20:32,970 --> 00:20:37,000
Its old tower-top looked over an all between the house and gates.

279
00:20:38,459 --> 00:20:44,500
I was yet enjoying the calm prospect and pleasant fresh air, yet listening with

280
00:20:44,501 --> 00:20:50,000
delight to the coreing of the rooks, yet surveying the wide, horry front of the

281
00:20:50,001 --> 00:20:56,180
hall, and thinking what a great place it was for one lonely little dame like Mrs.

282
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:59,680
Fairfax to inhabit, when that lady appeared at the door.

283
00:21:00,319 --> 00:21:01,319
What?

284
00:21:01,509 --> 00:21:03,459
Out already, said she.

285
00:21:04,180 --> 00:21:05,799
I see you are an early riser.

286
00:21:06,970 --> 00:21:11,139
I went up to her and was received with an affable kiss and shake of the hand.

287
00:21:12,490 --> 00:21:15,080
How do you like thornfield, she asked?

288
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:17,180
I told her I liked it very much.

289
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:19,340
Yes, she said.

290
00:21:20,159 --> 00:21:26,500
It is a pretty place, but I fear it will be getting out of order, unless Mr.

291
00:21:26,599 --> 00:21:30,340
Rochester should take it into his head to come and reside here permanently.

292
00:21:31,580 --> 00:21:34,220
Or at least visit it rather often.

293
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,319
Great houses and fine grounds require the presence of the proprietor.

294
00:21:40,979 --> 00:21:43,380
Mr. Rochester, I exclaimed.

295
00:21:43,840 --> 00:21:44,840
Who is he?

296
00:21:45,689 --> 00:21:48,720
The owner of Thornfield, she responded quietly.

297
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:52,560
Did you not know he was called Rochester?

298
00:21:53,650 --> 00:21:54,650
Of course, I did not.

299
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:56,319
I had never heard of him before.

300
00:21:57,319 --> 00:22:01,819
But the old lady seemed to regard his existence as a universally understood

301
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:06,020
fact, with which everybody must be acquainted by instinct.

302
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:09,419
I thought I continued.

303
00:22:10,299 --> 00:22:11,759
Thornfield belonged to you.

304
00:22:12,619 --> 00:22:13,619
To me.

305
00:22:14,180 --> 00:22:16,000
Bless you, child, what an idea.

306
00:22:16,900 --> 00:22:17,900
To me.

307
00:22:18,299 --> 00:22:20,819
I am only the housekeeper, the manager.

308
00:22:21,619 --> 00:22:26,340
To be sure, I am distantly related to the Rochester's by the mother's side,

309
00:22:26,909 --> 00:22:32,240
or at least my husband was, he was a clergyman, incumbent of hay, that little

310
00:22:32,241 --> 00:22:36,619
village yonder on the hill, and that church near the gates was his.

311
00:22:38,119 --> 00:22:44,040
The present Mr. Rochester's mother was a fairfax and second cousin to my husband.

312
00:22:45,220 --> 00:22:47,240
But I never presume on the connection.

313
00:22:48,319 --> 00:22:50,500
In fact, it is nothing to me.

314
00:22:51,299 --> 00:22:54,779
I consider myself quite in the light of an ordinary housekeeper.

315
00:22:55,799 --> 00:23:01,060
My employer is always civil, and I expect nothing more.

316
00:23:02,970 --> 00:23:06,020
And the little girl, my pupil.

317
00:23:07,709 --> 00:23:09,319
She is Mr. Rochester's ward.

318
00:23:10,119 --> 00:23:12,259
He commissioned me to find a governess for her.

319
00:23:13,099 --> 00:23:17,520
He intended to have her brought up in a shire, I believe.

320
00:23:18,669 --> 00:23:22,419
Here she comes, with her born, as she calls her nurse.

321
00:23:23,784 --> 00:23:25,340
The enigma then was explained.

322
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:32,720
This affable and kind little widow was no great dame, but a dependent like myself.

323
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:35,520
I did not like her the worst for that.

324
00:23:35,939 --> 00:23:39,259
On the contrary, I felt better pleased than ever.

325
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:42,520
The equality between her and me was real.

326
00:23:43,240 --> 00:23:45,840
Not the mere result of condescension on her part.

327
00:23:46,659 --> 00:23:47,959
So much the better.

328
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:51,479
My position was all the freer.

329
00:23:53,150 --> 00:23:57,540
As I was meditating on this discovery, a little girl, followed by her attendant,

330
00:23:58,110 --> 00:23:59,319
came running up the lawn.

331
00:24:00,509 --> 00:24:04,020
I looked at my pupil, who did not at first appear to notice me.

332
00:24:05,130 --> 00:24:11,040
She was quite a child, perhaps seven or eight years old, slightly built,

333
00:24:11,634 --> 00:24:14,621
with a pale, small featured face, and a

334
00:24:14,622 --> 00:24:18,280
redundancy of hair falling in curls to her waist.

335
00:24:19,575 --> 00:24:22,500
Good morning, Miss Adela, said Mrs. Fairfax.

336
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:28,241
Come and speak to the lady who is to teach you and to make you a clever woman someday.

337
00:24:28,799 --> 00:24:29,799
She approached.

338
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:37,000
Se la maguva nante said she, pointing to me and addressing her nurse, who answered,

339
00:24:37,849 --> 00:24:39,639
Maze-Wi, set en mort.

340
00:24:41,259 --> 00:24:42,259
Are they foreigners?

341
00:24:43,119 --> 00:24:46,200
I inquired, amazed at hearing the French language.

342
00:24:47,125 --> 00:24:50,240
The nurse is a foreigner, and Adela was born on the continent.

343
00:24:51,330 --> 00:24:55,060
And I believe, never left it till within six months ago.

344
00:24:56,189 --> 00:24:58,759
When she first came here, she could speak no English.

345
00:24:59,700 --> 00:25:02,540
Now she can make shift to talk it a little.

346
00:25:03,720 --> 00:25:04,959
I don't understand her.

347
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:09,720
She mixes it so with French, but you will make out her meaning very well,

348
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:10,860
I dare say.

349
00:25:12,680 --> 00:25:18,439
Fortunately, I had had the advantage of being taught French by a French lady.

350
00:25:19,930 --> 00:25:25,080
And as I had always made a point of conversing with Madame Piero, as often as

351
00:25:25,080 --> 00:25:28,736
I could, and had besides, during the last seven

352
00:25:28,737 --> 00:25:32,561
years, learnt a portion of French by heart daily.

353
00:25:33,830 --> 00:25:38,540
Applying myself to take pains with my accent and imitating as closely as

354
00:25:38,541 --> 00:25:44,040
possible the pronunciation of my teacher, I had acquired a certain degree of

355
00:25:44,041 --> 00:25:48,198
readiness and correctness in the language, and was not

356
00:25:48,199 --> 00:25:51,919
likely to be much at a loss with Mademoiselle Adela.

357
00:25:53,509 --> 00:25:57,799
She came and shook hands with me when she heard that I was her governess,

358
00:25:58,659 --> 00:26:05,501
and as I led her into breakfast, I addressed some phrases to her in her own tongue.

359
00:26:05,810 --> 00:26:10,700
She replied briefly at first, but after we were seated at the table.

360
00:26:11,439 --> 00:26:15,619
And she had examined me some ten minutes with her large hazel eyes.

361
00:26:16,409 --> 00:26:18,919
She suddenly commenced chattering fluently.

362
00:26:20,450 --> 00:26:22,939
Ah, cried she in French.

363
00:26:23,919 --> 00:26:27,060
You speak my language as well as Mr. Rochester does.

364
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:30,700
I can talk to you as I can to him and so can Sophie.

365
00:26:31,720 --> 00:26:35,000
She will be glad, nobody here understands her.

366
00:26:36,040 --> 00:26:37,819
Madem Fairfax is all English.

367
00:26:38,619 --> 00:26:39,619
Sophie is my nurse.

368
00:26:40,560 --> 00:26:44,340
She came with me over the sea in a great ship with a chimney that smoked,

369
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:50,259
how it did smoke, and I was sick, and so was Sophie, and so was Mr.

370
00:26:50,419 --> 00:26:51,419
Rochester.

371
00:26:51,779 --> 00:26:56,520
Mr. Rochester lay down on a sofa in a pretty room called the salon.

372
00:26:57,490 --> 00:27:00,060
And Sophie and I had little beds in another place.

373
00:27:01,049 --> 00:27:02,479
I nearly fell out of mine.

374
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:04,240
It was like a shelf.

375
00:27:05,470 --> 00:27:07,119
And Mademoiselle, what is your name?

376
00:27:08,880 --> 00:27:10,639
Eyre, Jane Eyre.

377
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:13,099
Eyre, bar, I cannot say it.

378
00:27:13,380 --> 00:27:17,099
Well, our ship stopped in the morning before it was quite daylight.

379
00:27:17,599 --> 00:27:22,560
At a great city, a huge city, with very dark houses and all smoky.

380
00:27:23,180 --> 00:27:26,020
Not at all like the pretty clean town I came from.

381
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:29,907
And Mr. Rochester carried me in his arms over

382
00:27:29,908 --> 00:27:33,580
a plank to the land, and Sophie came after.

383
00:27:33,709 --> 00:27:36,915
And we all got into a coach, which took us to a

384
00:27:36,916 --> 00:27:40,400
beautiful large house, larger than this and finer.

385
00:27:41,259 --> 00:27:42,259
Called in Hotel.

386
00:27:43,369 --> 00:27:44,919
We stayed there nearly a week.

387
00:27:45,560 --> 00:27:48,044
Eyre and Sophie used to walk every day in a

388
00:27:48,044 --> 00:27:51,561
great green place full of trees called the park.

389
00:27:52,169 --> 00:27:57,380
And there were many children there besides me, and a pond with beautiful birds in it

390
00:27:57,830 --> 00:27:58,979
that I fed with crumbs.

391
00:28:00,689 --> 00:28:03,680
Can you understand her when she runs on so fast?

392
00:28:04,299 --> 00:28:05,479
Asked Mrs. Fairfax.

393
00:28:06,959 --> 00:28:09,100
I understood her very well, for I had been

394
00:28:09,101 --> 00:28:12,741
accustomed to the fluent tongue of Madame Piero.

395
00:28:14,259 --> 00:28:16,680
I wish continued the good lady.

396
00:28:17,619 --> 00:28:20,119
You would ask her a question or two about her parents.

397
00:28:21,250 --> 00:28:22,919
I wonder if she remembers them.

398
00:28:24,064 --> 00:28:26,886
Adele, I inquired, with whom did you live when

399
00:28:26,887 --> 00:28:30,080
you were in that pretty clean town you spoke of.

400
00:28:31,439 --> 00:28:35,279
I lived long ago with Mama, but she has gone to the Holy Virgin.

401
00:28:36,180 --> 00:28:39,779
Mama used to teach me to dance and sing and to say verses.

402
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:46,220
A great many gentlemen and ladies came to see Mama, and I used to dance before them,

403
00:28:46,819 --> 00:28:49,080
or to sit on their knees and sing to them.

404
00:28:49,479 --> 00:28:50,479
I liked it.

405
00:28:50,979 --> 00:28:52,779
Shall I let you hear me sing now?

406
00:28:54,319 --> 00:28:57,356
She had finished her breakfast, so I permitted

407
00:28:57,358 --> 00:28:59,901
her to give a specimen of her accomplishments.

408
00:29:00,990 --> 00:29:06,020
Descending from her chair, she came and placed herself on my knee.

409
00:29:07,110 --> 00:29:13,700
Then, folding her little hands demurely before her, shaking back her curls and

410
00:29:13,701 --> 00:29:19,820
lifting her eyes to the ceiling, she commenced singing a song from some opera.

411
00:29:19,979 --> 00:29:25,819
It was the strain of a forsaken lady who, after bewailing the perfody of her lover,

412
00:29:26,560 --> 00:29:28,380
calls pride to her aid.

413
00:29:29,419 --> 00:29:34,500
Desires her attendant to deck her in her brightest jewels and richest robes,

414
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:41,319
and resolves to meet the false one that night at a ball, and proved to him by the

415
00:29:41,320 --> 00:29:46,439
guayety of her demeanor, how little his desertion has affected her.

416
00:29:47,400 --> 00:29:51,779
The subject seemed strangely chosen for an infant singer.

417
00:29:53,174 --> 00:29:57,959
But I suppose the point of the exhibition, lay in hearing the notes of love and

418
00:29:57,960 --> 00:30:02,078
jealousy warbled with the lisp of childhood, and in

419
00:30:02,079 --> 00:30:06,240
very bad taste that point was, at least I thought so.

420
00:30:07,159 --> 00:30:12,400
Adele sang the canzonette tunefully enough, and with the naivete of her age,

421
00:30:13,020 --> 00:30:16,514
this achieved, she jumped from my knee and said,

422
00:30:16,515 --> 00:30:19,980
now Mamoiselle, I will repeat you some poetry.

423
00:30:21,264 --> 00:30:26,979
Assuming an attitude, she began, la Ligue des rares, fabler de la

424
00:30:26,980 --> 00:30:32,799
frontaine, she then declaimed the little piece with an attention to punctuation and

425
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:39,580
emphasis, a flexibility of voice and an appropriateness of gesture, very unusual

426
00:30:39,580 --> 00:30:44,860
indeed at her age, and which proved she had been carefully trained.

427
00:30:46,639 --> 00:30:48,560
Was it your mamma who taught you that piece?

428
00:30:48,979 --> 00:30:49,979
I asked.

429
00:30:50,580 --> 00:30:53,336
Yes, and she just used to say it in this way,

430
00:30:53,337 --> 00:30:57,500
quave-voudonc, Louis-Diton de ses rats, parlés.

431
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:03,840
She made me lift my hand, so, to remind me to raise my voice at the question.

432
00:31:04,500 --> 00:31:05,540
Now shall I dance for you?

433
00:31:07,060 --> 00:31:08,419
No, that will do.

434
00:31:09,290 --> 00:31:11,679
But after your mamma went to the Holy Virgin,

435
00:31:11,680 --> 00:31:14,960
as you say, with whom did you live then?

436
00:31:16,099 --> 00:31:19,470
With Madame Frederick and her husband, she took

437
00:31:19,471 --> 00:31:22,620
care of me, but she is nothing related to me.

438
00:31:23,139 --> 00:31:27,720
I think she is poor, for she had not so fine a house as mamma.

439
00:31:29,500 --> 00:31:30,540
I was not long there.

440
00:31:31,919 --> 00:31:34,880
Mr. Rochester asked me if I would like to go and live with him in England,

441
00:31:36,130 --> 00:31:37,130
and I said yes.

442
00:31:38,860 --> 00:31:44,139
For I knew Mr. Rochester before I knew Madame Frederick, and he was always kind

443
00:31:44,140 --> 00:31:50,200
to me and gave me pretty dresses and toys, but you see, he has not kept his word,

444
00:31:51,150 --> 00:31:55,959
for he has brought me to England, and now he has gone back again himself,

445
00:31:56,790 --> 00:31:57,939
and I never see him.

446
00:31:59,500 --> 00:32:02,840
After breakfast, Adele and I withdrew to the library.

447
00:32:03,659 --> 00:32:05,139
Which room it appears?

448
00:32:06,049 --> 00:32:09,479
Mr. Rochester had directed should be used as the school room.

449
00:32:10,779 --> 00:32:17,040
Most of the books were locked up behind glass doors, but there was one bookcase

450
00:32:17,041 --> 00:32:23,700
left open containing everything that could be needed in the way of elementary works,

451
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:27,359
and several volumes of light literature,

452
00:32:27,360 --> 00:32:31,139
poetry, biography, travels, a few romances, etc.

453
00:32:33,049 --> 00:32:36,013
I suppose he had considered that these were all the

454
00:32:36,014 --> 00:32:38,700
governess would require for her private perusal.

455
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:44,099
And indeed, they contended me amply for the present.

456
00:32:45,849 --> 00:32:49,500
Compared with the scanty pickings I had now and then been able to glean at

457
00:32:49,501 --> 00:32:55,040
Lowwood, they seemed to offer an abundant harvest of entertainment and information.

458
00:32:56,979 --> 00:33:02,919
In this room, too, there was a cabinet piano, quite new and of superior tone,

459
00:33:03,380 --> 00:33:06,939
also an easel for painting and a pair of globes.

460
00:33:07,849 --> 00:33:13,360
I found my pupils sufficiently docile, though disinclined to apply, she had not

461
00:33:13,361 --> 00:33:15,540
been used to regular occupation of any kind.

462
00:33:16,834 --> 00:33:21,139
I felt it would be injudicious to confine her too much at first.

463
00:33:22,080 --> 00:33:26,959
So, when I had talked to her a great deal and got her to learn a little,

464
00:33:27,930 --> 00:33:32,900
and when the morning had advanced to noon, I allowed her to return to her nurse.

465
00:33:34,084 --> 00:33:36,525
I then proposed to occupy myself till dinner

466
00:33:36,526 --> 00:33:39,500
time, in drawing some little sketches for her use.

467
00:33:40,950 --> 00:33:46,640
As I was going upstairs to fetch my portfolio and pencils, Mrs. Fairfax called to me.

468
00:33:47,540 --> 00:33:51,299
Your morning school hours are over now, I suppose, said she.

469
00:33:52,430 --> 00:33:55,639
She was in a room, the folding doors of which stood open.

470
00:33:56,729 --> 00:33:58,500
I went in when she addressed me.

471
00:33:59,580 --> 00:34:03,619
It was a large stately apartment, with purple chairs and curtains,

472
00:34:04,479 --> 00:34:10,740
a turkey carpet, walnut-panelled walls, one vast window rich in stained glass,

473
00:34:11,400 --> 00:34:13,919
and a lofty ceiling nobly molded.

474
00:34:14,400 --> 00:34:19,960
Mrs. Fairfax was dusting some vases of fine purple spa which stood on a sideboard.

475
00:34:20,680 --> 00:34:21,900
What a beautiful room!

476
00:34:22,599 --> 00:34:29,701
I exclaimed, as I looked around, for I had never before seen any half so imposing.

477
00:34:31,619 --> 00:34:33,560
Yes, this is the dining room.

478
00:34:36,200 --> 00:34:39,460
I have little air in sunshine, for everything gets

479
00:34:39,461 --> 00:34:42,380
so damp in apartments that are seldom inhabited.

480
00:34:43,250 --> 00:34:45,400
The drawing-room yonder feels like a vault.

481
00:34:47,020 --> 00:34:52,479
She pointed to a wide arch corresponding to the window, and hung like it with a

482
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:58,979
tyrann -died curtain, now looped up, mounting to it by two broad steps,

483
00:34:59,740 --> 00:35:00,740
and looking through.

484
00:35:01,209 --> 00:35:06,119
I thought I caught a glimpse of a fairy place, so bright to my novice eyes,

485
00:35:06,419 --> 00:35:07,779
appeared the view beyond.

486
00:35:09,119 --> 00:35:14,580
Yet, it was merely a very pretty drawing -room, and within it, a budwah,

487
00:35:15,340 --> 00:35:19,900
both spread with white carpets, on which seemed laid brilliant garlands of

488
00:35:19,901 --> 00:35:25,799
flowers, both sealed with snowy moldings of white grapes and vine leaves,

489
00:35:26,580 --> 00:35:31,500
beneath which glowed in rich contrast crimson couches and ottomans, while the

490
00:35:31,501 --> 00:35:35,900
ornaments on the pale perian mantelpiece were of sparkling bohemian glass,

491
00:35:36,439 --> 00:35:39,848
ruby red, and between the windows, large mirrors,

492
00:35:39,849 --> 00:35:43,400
repeated the general blending of snow and fire.

493
00:35:45,110 --> 00:35:50,500
In what order you keep these rooms, Mrs. Fairfax, said I, no dust,

494
00:35:50,919 --> 00:35:54,299
no canvas coverings, except that the air feels chilly.

495
00:35:55,200 --> 00:35:57,310
One would think they were inhabited daily.

496
00:35:58,369 --> 00:36:03,669
Why, Miss Eyre, though Mr. Rochester's visits here are rare, they are always

497
00:36:03,670 --> 00:36:08,990
sudden and unexpected, and as I observed that it put him out to find everything

498
00:36:08,991 --> 00:36:12,615
swayed up, and to have a bustle of arrangement on his

499
00:36:12,616 --> 00:36:15,849
arrival, I thought it best to keep the rooms in readiness.

500
00:36:17,650 --> 00:36:21,189
Is Mr. Rochester an exacting fastidious sort of man?

501
00:36:22,709 --> 00:36:27,450
Not particularly so, but he has a gentleman's taste and habits, and he

502
00:36:27,451 --> 00:36:30,290
expects to have things managed in conformity to them.

503
00:36:31,700 --> 00:36:32,700
Do you like him?

504
00:36:32,930 --> 00:36:33,930
Is he generally liked?

505
00:36:34,869 --> 00:36:40,689
Oh yes, the family have always been respected here, almost all the land in

506
00:36:40,690 --> 00:36:43,229
this neighborhood, as far as you can see, has

507
00:36:43,230 --> 00:36:46,611
belonged to the Rochester's time out of mind.

508
00:36:47,630 --> 00:36:52,290
Well, but, leaving his land out of the question, do you like him?

509
00:36:52,889 --> 00:36:53,909
Is he liked for himself?

510
00:36:55,415 --> 00:37:00,909
I have no cause to do otherwise than like him, and I believe he is considered a just

511
00:37:00,911 --> 00:37:05,630
and liberal landlord by his tenants, but he has never lived much amongst them.

512
00:37:06,869 --> 00:37:08,750
But has he no peculiarities?

513
00:37:09,509 --> 00:37:11,470
What, in short, is his character?

514
00:37:13,409 --> 00:37:16,090
Oh, his character is unimpeachable, I suppose.

515
00:37:17,060 --> 00:37:19,110
He is rather peculiar, perhaps.

516
00:37:19,889 --> 00:37:25,209
He has travelled a great deal, and seen a great deal of the world, I should think.

517
00:37:25,849 --> 00:37:30,169
I dare say he is clever, but I never had much conversation with him.

518
00:37:31,150 --> 00:37:32,810
In what way is he peculiar?

519
00:37:33,889 --> 00:37:34,889
I don't know.

520
00:37:35,490 --> 00:37:41,111
It is not easy to describe nothing striking, but you feel it when he speaks to you.

521
00:37:41,169 --> 00:37:44,398
You cannot be always sure whether he is ingest or

522
00:37:44,399 --> 00:37:47,529
earnest, whether he is pleased or the contrary.

523
00:37:48,509 --> 00:37:52,330
You don't thoroughly understand him in short, at least I don't.

524
00:37:53,194 --> 00:37:57,229
But it is of no consequence, he is a very good master.

525
00:37:59,584 --> 00:38:04,810
This was all the account I got from Mrs Fairfax of her employer and mine.

526
00:38:05,939 --> 00:38:09,310
There are people who seem to have no notion of sketching a character,

527
00:38:09,990 --> 00:38:15,389
or observing and describing salient points, either in persons or things.

528
00:38:16,430 --> 00:38:19,450
The good lady evidently belonged to this class.

529
00:38:20,639 --> 00:38:23,349
My queries puzzled, but did not draw her out.

530
00:38:24,590 --> 00:38:27,830
Mr. Rochester was Mr. Rochester in her eyes.

531
00:38:28,694 --> 00:38:32,069
A gentleman, a landed proprietor, nothing more.

532
00:38:32,939 --> 00:38:37,167
She inquired and searched no further, and evidently wondered

533
00:38:37,168 --> 00:38:41,169
at my wish to gain a more definite notion of his identity.

534
00:38:42,599 --> 00:38:47,009
When we left the dining room, she proposed to show me over the rest of the house,

535
00:38:47,819 --> 00:38:52,669
and I followed her upstairs and downstairs, admiring as I went,

536
00:38:53,400 --> 00:38:56,029
for all was well arranged and handsome.

537
00:38:57,200 --> 00:39:02,310
The large front chambers I thought especially grand, and some of the third

538
00:39:02,311 --> 00:39:07,751
story rooms, though dark and low, were interesting from their air of antiquity.

539
00:39:08,590 --> 00:39:11,770
The furniture once appropriated to the lower apartments had,

540
00:39:11,771 --> 00:39:15,229
from time to time, been removed here as fashions changed.

541
00:39:16,009 --> 00:39:20,610
And the imperfect light entering by their narrow casements showed bedsteads of a

542
00:39:20,611 --> 00:39:26,369
hundred years old, chests in oak or walnut, looking with their strange

543
00:39:26,370 --> 00:39:31,389
carvings of palm branches and cherubs' heads, like types of the Hebrew Ark,

544
00:39:32,229 --> 00:39:38,529
rows of venerable chairs, high-backed and narrow, stools still more antiquated,

545
00:39:39,009 --> 00:39:43,529
on whose cushion tops were yet apparent traces of half-a-faced embroideries,

546
00:39:44,130 --> 00:39:48,110
wrought by fingers that for two generations had been coffin dust.

547
00:39:48,970 --> 00:39:52,672
All these relics gave to the third story of Thornfield

548
00:39:52,673 --> 00:39:57,389
Hall, the aspect of a home of the past, a shrine of memory.

549
00:39:58,810 --> 00:40:03,450
I liked the hush, the gloom, the quaintness of these retreats in the day,

550
00:40:04,709 --> 00:40:09,569
but I by no means coveted a night's repose on one of those wide and heavy beds,

551
00:40:10,240 --> 00:40:17,169
shut in some of them, with doors of oak, shaded others, with wrought-old English

552
00:40:17,170 --> 00:40:22,590
hangings crusted with thick work, portraying effigies of strange flowers and

553
00:40:22,591 --> 00:40:28,029
stranger birds and strangest human beings, all which would have looked strange indeed

554
00:40:28,529 --> 00:40:30,610
by the pallid gleam of moonlight.

555
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:33,830
Do the servants sleep in these rooms?

556
00:40:34,509 --> 00:40:35,509
I asked.

557
00:40:35,930 --> 00:40:40,029
No, they occupy a range of smaller apartments to the back.

558
00:40:40,930 --> 00:40:42,130
No one ever sleeps here.

559
00:40:42,950 --> 00:40:45,844
One would almost say that, if there were a ghost

560
00:40:45,845 --> 00:40:49,050
at Thornfield Hall, this would be its haunt.

561
00:40:50,119 --> 00:40:52,569
So I think, you have no ghost, then?

562
00:40:53,939 --> 00:40:58,905
None that I ever heard of, returned Mrs. Fairfax smiling,

563
00:40:58,906 --> 00:41:03,290
nor any traditions of one, no legends or ghost stories.

564
00:41:04,959 --> 00:41:09,648
I believe not, and yet it is said the Rochester's have

565
00:41:09,649 --> 00:41:13,470
been rather a violent than a quiet race in their time.

566
00:41:14,354 --> 00:41:19,349
Perhaps, though, that is the reason they rest tranquilly in their graves now.

567
00:41:20,590 --> 00:41:25,389
Yes, after life's fitful fever, they sleep well, I muttered.

568
00:41:26,389 --> 00:41:30,110
Where are you going now, Mrs. Fairfax, for she was moving away?

569
00:41:31,479 --> 00:41:34,790
On to the leads, will you come and see the view from thence?

570
00:41:36,950 --> 00:41:42,849
I followed still, up a very narrow staircase to the Attics, and thence by a

571
00:41:42,850 --> 00:41:46,150
ladder and through a trap door to the roof of the hall.

572
00:41:47,630 --> 00:41:52,610
I was now on a level with the crow-colony, and could see into their nests.

573
00:41:53,750 --> 00:41:56,554
Leaning over the battlements and looking far

574
00:41:56,555 --> 00:42:00,091
down, I surveyed the grounds laid out like a map.

575
00:42:00,830 --> 00:42:05,229
The bright and velvet lawn, closely girdling the gray base of the mansion.

576
00:42:06,110 --> 00:42:10,409
The field, wide as a park, dotted with its ancient timber.

577
00:42:11,139 --> 00:42:16,006
The wood, done and serr, divided by a path visibly overgrown,

578
00:42:16,007 --> 00:42:19,090
greener with moss than the trees were with foliage.

579
00:42:20,169 --> 00:42:23,187
The church at the gates, the road, the tranquil

580
00:42:23,187 --> 00:42:25,830
hills, all reposing in the autumn day's sun.

581
00:42:26,750 --> 00:42:32,950
The horizon bounded by a propitious sky, azure, marbled with pearly white.

582
00:42:33,770 --> 00:42:37,729
No feature in the scene was extraordinary, but all was pleasing.

583
00:42:40,270 --> 00:42:43,148
When I turned from it and repast the trap door,

584
00:42:43,149 --> 00:42:46,710
I could scarcely see my way down the ladder.

585
00:42:47,250 --> 00:42:51,330
The Attics seemed black as a vault, compared with that arch of blue air to

586
00:42:51,330 --> 00:42:56,389
which I had been looking up, and to that sunlit scene of grove, pasture and green

587
00:42:56,390 --> 00:43:03,271
hill, of which the hall was the centre, and over which I had been gazing with delight.

588
00:43:04,779 --> 00:43:08,729
Mrs Fairfax stayed behind a moment to fasten the trap door.

589
00:43:09,610 --> 00:43:13,735
I, by dint of groping, found the outlet from the attic,

590
00:43:13,736 --> 00:43:17,049
and proceeded to descend the narrow Garrett staircase.

591
00:43:19,000 --> 00:43:24,529
I lingered in the long passage to which this led, separating the front and back

592
00:43:24,530 --> 00:43:30,130
rooms of the third story, narrow, low and dim, with only one little window

593
00:43:30,131 --> 00:43:36,069
at the far end, and looking, with its two rows of small black doors all shut,

594
00:43:36,669 --> 00:43:38,790
like a corridor in some blue-beard's castle.

595
00:43:40,349 --> 00:43:45,889
While I paced softly on, the last sound I expected to hear in so still a region,

596
00:43:46,409 --> 00:43:48,409
a laugh struck my ear.

597
00:43:49,389 --> 00:43:53,806
It was a curious laugh, distinct, formal, merciless,

598
00:43:53,905 --> 00:43:57,650
I stopped, the sound ceased, only for an instant.

599
00:43:58,430 --> 00:43:59,810
It began again, louder.

600
00:44:00,729 --> 00:44:03,990
For at first, though distinct, it was very low.

601
00:44:04,970 --> 00:44:08,869
It passed off in a clamorous peel that seemed to wake an echo in every lonely

602
00:44:08,870 --> 00:44:12,588
chamber, though it originated but in one, and I could

603
00:44:12,590 --> 00:44:16,189
have pointed out the door once the accents issued.

604
00:44:18,069 --> 00:44:23,689
Mrs. Fairfax, I called out, for I now heard her descending the great stairs.

605
00:44:24,575 --> 00:44:25,909
Did you hear that loud laugh?

606
00:44:26,849 --> 00:44:27,849
Who is it?

607
00:44:28,909 --> 00:44:32,650
Some of the servants very likely, she answered, perhaps Grace Paul.

608
00:44:34,369 --> 00:44:35,369
Did you hear it?

609
00:44:35,650 --> 00:44:36,650
I again inquired.

610
00:44:37,290 --> 00:44:38,290
Yes, plainly.

611
00:44:38,750 --> 00:44:39,770
I often hear her.

612
00:44:40,150 --> 00:44:42,876
She sows in one of these rooms, sometimes liars

613
00:44:42,877 --> 00:44:44,970
with her, they are frequently noisy together.

614
00:44:46,060 --> 00:44:52,069
The laugh was repeated in its low syllabic tone, and terminated in an odd murmur.

615
00:44:53,580 --> 00:44:56,349
Grace exclaimed Mrs. Fairfax.

616
00:44:57,630 --> 00:45:01,463
I really did not expect any Grace to answer, for the laugh

617
00:45:01,465 --> 00:45:05,790
was as tragic as preternatural a laugh as any I ever heard.

618
00:45:07,319 --> 00:45:12,209
And, but that it was high noon, and that no circumstance of ghostliness

619
00:45:12,210 --> 00:45:17,229
accompanied the curious cacunation, but that neither scene nor season favored

620
00:45:17,230 --> 00:45:20,330
fear, I should have been superstitiously afraid.

621
00:45:21,509 --> 00:45:26,590
However, the event showed me I was a fool for entertaining a sense even of surprise.

622
00:45:27,825 --> 00:45:33,470
The door nearest me opened, and a servant came out, a woman of between 30 and 40,

623
00:45:34,369 --> 00:45:40,849
a set, square-made figure, red-haired, and with a hard plain face any apparition

624
00:45:40,850 --> 00:45:44,529
less romantic or less ghostly could scarcely be conceived.

625
00:45:46,194 --> 00:45:50,729
Too much noise Grace said Mrs. Fairfax, remember directions.

626
00:45:52,080 --> 00:45:54,169
Grace curtsied silently and went in.

627
00:45:55,450 --> 00:45:58,096
She is a person we have to so and assist Leia

628
00:45:58,097 --> 00:46:01,710
in her housemaids work, continued the widow.

629
00:46:01,990 --> 00:46:06,470
Not altogether unobjectionable in some points, but she does well enough.

630
00:46:07,389 --> 00:46:08,389
By the by.

631
00:46:09,020 --> 00:46:11,409
How have you got on with your new pupil this morning?

632
00:46:13,279 --> 00:46:17,176
The conversation, thus turned on Adele, continued

633
00:46:17,177 --> 00:46:20,190
till we reached the light and cheerful region below.

634
00:46:21,330 --> 00:46:26,689
Adele came running to meet us in the hall, exclaiming Mesdame-Vouset-Servis-Adding,

635
00:46:27,189 --> 00:46:28,849
Jaibien-Faim-Mois.

636
00:46:30,389 --> 00:46:34,729
We found dinner ready, and waiting for us in Mrs. Fairfax's room.
